### render
cicada render <score.cicada> -o <out.wav> [flags]

render a score to WAV

Flags:
  -o <file>                    required output WAV path
  --rate <Hz>                  sample rate (default 48000)
  --bits <16|24|32>            WAV bit depth (default 24)
  --from <bar>                 one-based start bar (default 1)
  --bars <n>                   bars to render; 0 means through song end
  --tail <duration>            render tail (default 3s)
  --dither=<bool>              deterministic TPDF dither for integer PCM (default true)
  --normalize=<bool>           peak normalize to -1 dBFS (default false)
  --block <frames>             offline block size (default 4096)
  --loudness <LUFS>            target integrated loudness
  --true-peak-max <dBTP>       maximum true peak for loudness rendering (default -1)
  --loudness-tolerance <LU>    target tolerance (default 0.5)
  --sampler-baseline           compare one recorded layer/take and 2 ms release
  --max-resident-pcm <size>    cap on decoded sampler-pack PCM held in memory, such as 512MiB (default 1GiB)
  --export <name>              use a named export profile
  --help                       show this help

Use --from 1 for the first bar. The legacy value --from 0 is accepted with a warning and means bar 1. Loudness targets (--loudness, or an export block with loudness) add a static gain. Without a master limiter the direct renderer applies it after the master chain, which keeps crest factor and can miss an ambitious target; the scheduled renderer (arrangements, audio clips, serial track effects, sample packs) applies it before the chain, as it always has. If the score's master chain contains a limiter, the gain goes in before the chain so that limiter holds the true-peak ceiling, capped at +12 dB of input gain and 6 dB of limiter gain reduction; a target that needs more is reported as a shortfall. Sampler packs stay in memory as decoded float32 PCM, and --max-resident-pcm caps their total; a project whose packs decode to more is refused before it renders, with the cap and the size measured in the message. CICADA_MAX_RESIDENT_PCM sets the cap when the option is omitted. Sizes are whole numbers with KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB, KB, MB, GB or TB, such as 512MiB.

### stems
cicada stems <score.cicada> -o <directory> [flags]

render separate float32 WAV stems

Flags:
  -o <directory>             required output directory
  --rate <Hz>                sample rate (default 48000)
  --bits 32                  float32 output only
  --from <bar>               one-based start bar (default 1)
  --bars <n>                 bars to render; 0 means through song end
  --tail <duration>          render tail (default 3s)
  --dither=<bool>            deterministic TPDF dither option
  --normalize=<bool>         peak normalization option
  --block <frames>           offline block size (default 4096)
  --max-resident-pcm <size>  cap on decoded sampler-pack PCM held in memory, such as 512MiB (default 1GiB)
  --help                     show this help

Use --from 1 for the first bar. The legacy value --from 0 is accepted with a warning and means bar 1.

### audition
cicada audition <score.cicada> [flags]

report what a score sounds like

Flags:
  -o <directory>             write spectrogram.png, chroma.png and rhythm.png
  --from <bar>               one-based start bar (default 1)
  --bars <n>                 bars to analyze; 0 means through song end
  --json                     write the report as JSON (format cicada.audition/1)
  --max-resident-pcm <size>  cap on decoded sampler-pack PCM held in memory, such as 2GiB (default 1GiB)
  --help                     show this help

Renders the mix and stems, then reports heard key, written and heard chords per bar, and per-track level, brightness, attack, decay and rhythm. See docs/manual/audition.md. CICADA_MAX_RESIDENT_PCM sets the sampler-pack cap when the option is omitted.

### verify-wav
cicada verify-wav <file.wav> [flags]

verify WAV samples and timing

Flags:
  --rate <Hz>                 expected sample rate (default 48000)
  --bits <16|24|32>           expected bit depth (default 24)
  --from <bar>                one-based start bar (default 1)
  --bars <n>                  expected bars (required)
  --tail <duration>           expected tail (default 3s)
  --peak-max-db <dBFS>        peak ceiling (default -0.3)
  --dc-max-db <dBFS>          DC ceiling (default -60)
  --lufs <LUFS>               integrated-loudness target
  --lufs-tolerance <LU>       loudness tolerance (default 0.5)
  --true-peak-max <dBTP>      true-peak ceiling
  --report                    write JSON report to stderr
  --help                      show this help

Use --from 1 for the first bar. The legacy value --from 0 is accepted with a warning and means bar 1.

### verify-stems
cicada verify-stems <directory> [flags]

verify a stem directory

Flags:
  --tap <name>             verification tap (default pre-comp)
  --residual-max-db <dBFS> maximum bus-sum residual (default -80)
  --help                   show this help

### wam2
cicada wam2 <score.cicada> -o <directory> [flags]

export a WAM2 instrument

Flags:
  -o <directory>  required output plugin directory
  --kernel <file>  kernel WASM path (default build/cicada-kernel.wasm)
  --sdk <directory> WAM SDK directory (default build/wam2-sdk/node_modules/@webaudiomodules/sdk)
  --midi-track <id> MIDI instrument track (default first supported track)
  --help          show this help

### director
cicada director <score.cicada> -o <director.json> [--rate <Hz>]

write the Game Director manifest for browser hosts

Flags:
  -o <file>    required output JSON path
  --rate <Hz>  manifest sample rate: 44100, 48000 or 96000 (default 48000)
  --help       show this help

Resolves the score's live block (states, macros, stingers and transitions) to the kernel IDs and frame counts that @cicada/game-director and the Go sdk/director package read. The audio context must run at the manifest's rate. A score without a live block, or with more than 16 tracks, is refused. An existing output file is replaced.

### midi
cicada midi <score.cicada> [flags]

export a Standard MIDI File

Flags:
  -o <file>       required output MIDI path
  --bars <n>      song bars; 0 means all (default 0)
  --pattern <id>  export only one pattern
  --report        write JSON report to stderr
  --help          show this help

